an", "Anti-Alme vote split between a well-funded independent and an underfunded Democratic nominee", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win at about one percent"], "rationale": "The polling is recent, plentiful, and unanimous: Alme sits in the mid-40s while Bankhead stays in the teens, and raters and markets agree the Republican side is overwhelmingly favored. A $3 million independent candidacy further divides the opposition in a state already leaning ten points Republican, leaving the Democratic nominee with no visible path.", "watch": "Any sign that the independent's support collapses and consolidates behind Bankhead, or a late swing in the national environment, is the main scenario that would narrow this race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Steve Daines (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Montana's open Senate seat strongly favors Republican Kurt Alme. Twelve polls since June, including surveys from both parties' sponsors and neutral firms, show Alme leading Democrat Alani Bankhead by roughly 14 to 30 points, with the Republican consistently in the mid-40s and Bankhead in the teens. Expert raters across the spectrum call the race Safe, Solid, or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats about a one percent chance of winning.\n\nAlme holds the fundraising edge among major-party nominees, with $1.4 million banked and backing from the U.S. Chamber, while outside groups have amplified his campaign. Independent Seth Bodnar has raised over $3 million and Libertarian Kyle Austin is on the ballot, dividing the anti-Alme electorate. Bankhead's own fundraising is minimal, leaving her reliant on outside spending. With the state leaning ten points Republican and no evidence of movement, the seat remains firmly in Republican hands.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0053, "margin": -15.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0066, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0028, "margin": -20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-